  "query_preview": ".. A mid-level lobe of vorticity will continue to break \noff of the departing low, spinning up another weak mid-level low \nacross the southwest today. This won't surmount to much, other than \nthe development of northeasterly winds across the forecast area. \nover the next couple days, this feature will continue to weaken and \nphase into the mean flow as westerly flow aloft establishes across \nthe area. \n\nWith ample clearing and height rises occurring across northern and \ncentral UT tonight, freezing temperatures appear likely across \nthe Sevier and Sanpete valleys, though higher elevation areas \nwithin Juab/Millard counties and southwestern UT may see brief \ndips below freezing as well. Light surface winds beneath \nestablishing high pressure will set the stage for a more robust \nradiational cooling setup tonight across the specified locations. \n\nWith westerly flow aloft, dry and calm conditions are forecast to \npersist into early next week. Winds will be light and variable \nbeneath clear skies, with a slight warming trend to near-normal \ntemperatures statewide. \n\nConfidence continues to increase regarding a system impacting the \nforecast area mid-week next week. Guidance is generally trending \nmore widespread with precipitation, though forcing remains modest \namong most ensemble members as the trough looks to amplify further \neast. Roughly ~30% of guidance has this occurring over UT, resulting \nin heavier mountain snowfall totals and lower elevation rainfall \ntotals, as well as bringing snow levels down markedly compared to \nother suites. As the last few forecasts have specified, this \nforecast will continue to change substantially over the coming\ndays.",
